														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2044740">
									

<p>We keep him alive for the same reason we keep all our (as Pig_catapult has referred to them) &quot;blessed with suck&quot; humanoid scips alive: because they are invaluable as a means of learning more anout their anomalous properties. Honestly, you won't be upvoting many humanoid scips if that's your definition of cruel in the context of this setting. ;)</p>
<p>There are scores of scips who are better off if they're allowed to kill themselves, or if we destroy them.</p>
<p>Missed a point you make:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After 42 years, haven't we/they learned all they can from and about him? (And from the Foundation's cold point of view, why waste the resources to keep him alive any more?)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No, we haven't. That's something I tried to imply: even though we've had this guy since 1972, we've never been able to reverse engineer GRU-P's process. Think about it: the Russians were far ahead of everyone, and that was 40 years ago. Imagine what would have happened if they hadn't pushed through and had sat down to work out what was going wrong?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>P.S. Is Merrick a reference to the Elephant Man?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And yes, you get Easter Egg brownie points. :)</p>

