			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2044757">
									

<blockquote>
<p>they are invaluable as a means of learning more anout their anomalous properties.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, yeah. I get that.</p>
<p>But &#8230;</p>
<p><em>Forty-two years</em>? Blinded, deafened, mute? Restrained?</p>
<p>How is he nourished? A puncture wound (an <em>intravenous</em> puncture wound) would start the tissue reaction. Does he have a naso-gastric tube? If so, add &quot;feeling like you are going to gag or choke all the time&quot; to the torment he is in.</p>
<p>What about elimination? Those burns would not only seal any wound, they would also seal natural bodily openings - so how do they get around that without causing more tissue proliferation?</p>
<p>The only one I can think of that is worse off is poor little 231, and she's <em>only</em> kept alive because of the end-of-the-world problem killing her or otherwise easing her misery would cause.</p>
<p>Maybe if you changed it from being restrained to being kept in an artificial coma. It would make more sense, and be more humane. He's alive to experiment on, but he's &quot;not home&quot; to be tortured.</p>
<p>I'm sorry, this totally a personal feeling here. But it just seems to be sadistic for no very good reason.</p>

