			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2044774">
									

<p>Well, feeding is obviously problematic. Without access to intravenous feeding or a feeding tube, all we can do is administer sustenance via drip feeding. When excising cauterized tissue, we make sure to keep his mouth accessible for that reason, because otherwise, yes, this would all end rather quickly. I could have added that to the containment procedures, but they are already lengthy and it seemed like inane detail in the context of the fictional format.</p>
<p>An artificial coma would be problematic for exactly the same reason as intravenous feeding would be problematic.</p>
<p>And your feelings are totally valid. To me this doesn't violate the &quot;cold, not cruel&quot; axiom, but that line is different for every reader. :)</p>

